// Shared definitions and conventions of the hvmc agent API.
//
// The coordinator (gRPC client, leader only) talks to agents (gRPC servers)
// over mutual TLS. Every agent serves CertificateService and grpc.health.v1;
// the host agent additionally serves HostAgent, each guest agent GuestAgent.
//
// Conventions that apply to every message in this package:
//
//   - Memory quantities are bytes, carried as int64 and never negative. Field
//     names end in `_bytes`. There are no percentages for memory amounts; the
//     only percentage is Hyper-V's own Dynamic Memory buffer setting.
//   - Every observation carries `observed_at` (google.protobuf.Timestamp, UTC),
//     the time the agent read the value from its source (Hyper-V,
//     /proc/meminfo, GlobalMemoryStatusEx), not the time the response was
//     sent. The coordinator treats values older than its `staleAfter` setting
//     as missing, so agents never refresh `observed_at` without re-reading.
//   - VMs are addressed by their Hyper-V name, the same name the host policy
//     (policy.proto) uses.
//
// Health (grpc.health.v1.Health, Check and Watch) is served on the same mTLS
// port. Service names: "" (overall), "hvmc.agent.v1.HostAgent" on the host
// agent, "hvmc.agent.v1.GuestAgent" on guest agents. NOT_SERVING means the
// agent's readiness checks fail; its data must then be treated as missing.
//
// Error model. Failures are reported as gRPC status codes; the status message
// is for humans and must not be parsed. The codes have these meanings:
//
//   - INVALID_ARGUMENT: the request is malformed (missing VM name, negative or
//     misaligned byte count, empty certificate chain, digest mismatch). Not
//     retryable without changing the request.
//   - NOT_FOUND: the named VM does not exist on the host.
//   - FAILED_PRECONDITION: the request is well-formed but the VM's state does
//     not allow it: a write to a `fixed` VM (read-only to the agent), a
//     Dynamic Memory direction violation (lowering the DM maximum or raising
//     the DM minimum), SetStartup on a DM VM or SetDynamicLimits on a static
//     VM, or InstallCertificate without a matching pending key. Not retryable.
//   - PERMISSION_DENIED: the agent's local policy denies the write: the VM is
//     not listed in the active policy, or the requested value lies outside the
//     policy's [min, max]. Not retryable until the operator activates a new
//     policy on the host.
//   - UNAUTHENTICATED: the client certificate's CN is not in the agent's
//     `allowedClients`. (Certificates not chained to the hvmc CA already fail
//     the TLS handshake.)
//   - RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED: the Hyper-V host does not have enough free memory
//     for the requested grow. Retryable once memory frees up.
//   - ABORTED: the guest refused (part of) a shrink. The VM may be partially
//     shrunk and its startup value may have changed anyway; the caller
//     re-reads the VM, retries at the midpoint (at most 3 attempts) and
//     repairs the startup value if needed.
//   - UNAVAILABLE: the agent cannot reach its data source right now: the
//     Hyper-V Virtual Machine Management service (vmms) is down or the
//     PowerShell worker is restarting. Retryable with backoff. Also used by
//     gRPC itself when the agent is unreachable.
//   - DEADLINE_EXCEEDED: the operation did not finish in time, either the
//     caller's deadline or the agent's per-cmdlet timeout (default 30 s). The
//     outcome of a write is unknown; the caller re-reads before retrying.
//   - UNIMPLEMENTED: the RPC is not served by this agent role.
//   - INTERNAL: an unexpected agent-side failure (bug, unparsable cmdlet
//     output). Retryable with backoff.
//
// When a write RPC fails after the agent touched the VM (ABORTED,
// DEADLINE_EXCEEDED, RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED), the agent attaches a ResizeResult
// to the status details if it could re-read the VM afterwards. Callers may
// use it for logging but must still re-read the VM themselves.

